# Line-ending normalisation. `text eol=lf` means: store LF in the repository, and
# check out LF regardless of the developer's core.autocrlf setting.

*.md text eol=lf

# *.js ADDED 2026-09-13, FOR ONE FILE AND ONE RECURRING BOT COMMIT.
#
# RRV8/config.js was the ONLY mixed-ending file in the repository -- measured with
# `git ls-files --eol`, which reports 456 blobs at i/lf, 130 binary, and exactly
# one at i/mixed. The 53 other tracked .js files were already i/lf, so this rule
# changes nothing about them beyond making the working copy match the blob.
#
# THE MIXED FILE IS NOT AN ACCIDENT OF EDITING. `Tools/build-ai-grounding.py`
# rewrites a marker-delimited block inside config.js using
# Path.write_text(newline=""), which writes exactly the bytes it was given and
# performs no newline translation. The block therefore carries whatever endings
# the generator emits while the rest of the file carries the repository's, and
# the two disagreed. Every run of the grounding bot then produced a 339-line
# diff that `git diff --ignore-cr-at-eol` shows to be carriage returns and
# nothing else -- a no-op commit, proposed forever.
#
# With this rule git normalises on staging, so a CR-only rewrite no longer
# reaches the index as a change and the bot stops proposing it. It does NOT
# change what the generator writes, and it is not a licence to drop the
# `newline=""` arguments: those exist so a CRLF working copy is not silently
# normalised on READ, which is a different problem in the other direction.
*.js text eol=lf
